course: dictionary
lesson: 16187
title: Strong beliefs and the word zealot
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A zealot is someone whose beliefs are so strong and fierce that they will not listen to anyone who disagrees. Context helps you find this meaning. «The zealot would not listen to anyone who disagreed with him.» The words would not listen show that this person is too stubborn, so zealot means someone who is too strong and fierce in their beliefs.
explain: Zealot can be used about religion or politics, so a news report might call someone a «religious zealot» or a «political zealot». In both cases the clue is the same: the person refuses to accept other views, and the belief matters more to them than anything else.
explain: Zealot has a second meaning in history. The Zealots were a group of Jewish people in the time of Jesus who fought against Roman rule. A capital Z shows you are reading about that group, so «Simon the Zealot» is a name that tells you more about the person.
fact: A zealot is a person who is too strong and fierce in their beliefs.
fact: Zealot is a noun, so you can write a zealot or zealots.
fact: A zealot can be about religion or politics.
fact: The Zealots were a Jewish group in the time of Jesus who fought against Roman rule.
fact: One of Jesus's disciples was called Simon the Zealot.
exercise: «The zealot would not listen to anyone who disagreed with him.» What does zealot mean here? | A person who loves to play football | A person who is always late | A kind teacher who helps everyone | *A person who is too fierce and strong in their beliefs
exercise: Our history book says the Zealots fought against Roman rule. Who were the Zealots? | A band of musicians | *A group of Jewish people in the time of Jesus who fought against Roman rule | A group of Roman soldiers | A team of farmers
exercise: Find and copy the word for a person who is too fierce in their beliefs. | river | teacher | *zealot | castle
test: The news called the man a religious zealot because he would not let anyone question his views. What does zealot mean? | *A person who is fierce, extreme and too strong about their beliefs | A person who is great at drawing | A person who is very tired after a long walk | A person who sells fruit at the market
test: Which phrase names one of Jesus's disciples? | Simon the Roman | Simon the Baker | *Simon the Zealot
exam: Which sentence uses zealot correctly? | The zealot planted tulips in the garden and watered them every day. | We went to the zealot to buy some bread and milk. | The zealot was a long, sleepy train that stopped at every village. | *Her uncle is such a zealot about his diet that he will not even taste a birthday cake.
